WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.339
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.639 --> 00:00:08.398
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.698 --> 00:00:16.900
Unit twenty-eight: Risk Society. In this unit, you will analyse how modern societies create and manage risk.

4
00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:19.349
risk society.

5
00:00:20.049 --> 00:00:27.470
risk society means Ulrich Beck's idea that modern societies are organised around managing self-created risks.

6
00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:31.709
Beck's risk society describes dangers we produce ourselves.

7
00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:35.665
Your turn. Say: risk society.

8
00:00:38.365 --> 00:00:39.644
risk society.

9
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.336
manufactured risk.

10
00:00:43.036 --> 00:00:48.611
manufactured risk means danger created by human activity rather than nature.

11
00:00:48.911 --> 00:00:51.631
Climate change is a manufactured risk.

12
00:00:52.331 --> 00:00:55.494
Your turn. Say: manufactured risk.

13
00:00:58.194 --> 00:00:59.648
manufactured risk.

14
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:01.953
systemic risk.

15
00:01:02.653 --> 00:01:07.323
systemic risk means the danger that one failure spreads through a whole system.

16
00:01:07.623 --> 00:01:10.899
The banking crisis exposed systemic risk.

17
00:01:11.599 --> 00:01:15.599
Your turn. Say: systemic risk.

18
00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:19.683
systemic risk.

19
00:01:20.683 --> 00:01:22.207
catastrophic risk.

20
00:01:22.907 --> 00:01:27.031
catastrophic risk means a danger that could cause enormous loss of life.

21
00:01:27.331 --> 00:01:29.644
Pandemics are a catastrophic risk.

22
00:01:30.344 --> 00:01:34.018
Your turn. Say: catastrophic risk.

23
00:01:36.718 --> 00:01:38.323
catastrophic risk.

24
00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:40.840
resilience thinking.

25
00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.804
resilience thinking means planning systems to absorb shocks and recover.

26
00:01:46.104 --> 00:01:49.299
Resilience thinking shapes modern flood defences.

27
00:01:49.999 --> 00:01:53.313
Your turn. Say: resilience thinking.

28
00:01:56.013 --> 00:01:57.525
resilience thinking.

29
00:01:58.517 --> 00:01:59.994
cascade failure.

30
00:02:00.694 --> 00:02:04.435
cascade failure means a chain reaction of failures.

31
00:02:04.735 --> 00:02:08.348
A power cut triggered a cascade failure across the network.

32
00:02:09.048 --> 00:02:12.211
Your turn. Say: cascade failure.

33
00:02:14.911 --> 00:02:16.330
cascade failure.

34
00:02:17.317 --> 00:02:19.142
precautionary politics.

35
00:02:19.842 --> 00:02:25.743
precautionary politics means policy that acts early to prevent uncertain harm.

36
00:02:26.043 --> 00:02:29.923
Precautionary politics guided the ban on certain pesticides.

37
00:02:30.623 --> 00:02:33.972
Your turn. Say: precautionary politics.

38
00:02:36.672 --> 00:02:38.555
precautionary politics.

39
00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:40.772
risk perception.

40
00:02:41.472 --> 00:02:46.339
risk perception means how people judge the seriousness of a danger.

41
00:02:46.639 --> 00:02:49.242
Media coverage shapes risk perception.

42
00:02:49.942 --> 00:02:52.943
Your turn. Say: risk perception.

43
00:02:55.643 --> 00:02:56.981
risk perception.

44
00:02:57.983 --> 00:03:00.203
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:00.503 --> 00:03:04.813
Ms. Sarah, why do modern societies seem so anxious about risk?

46
00:03:06.117 --> 00:03:13.085
Ulrich Beck argued that we now live in a risk society, wherein the gravest dangers are of our own making.

47
00:03:14.383 --> 00:03:17.172
Manufactured risks, like climate change?

48
00:03:18.483 --> 00:03:23.492
Indeed. And interconnected systems render us vulnerable to cascade failure.

49
00:03:24.783 --> 00:03:28.176
A single bank collapse spreading worldwide, for instance.

50
00:03:29.483 --> 00:03:32.447
Precisely, which is what is meant by systemic risk.

51
00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:36.899
Yet our risk perception is often distorted.

52
00:03:38.183 --> 00:03:45.340
Quite so. Hence the case, notwithstanding its costs, for resilience thinking and prudent precaution.

53
00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:51.517
Grammar focus: Formal and literary register (extended).

54
00:03:51.817 --> 00:03:55.361
Formal register uses elevated connectors and verbs.

55
00:03:55.661 --> 00:04:02.377
A society wherein the gravest dangers are self-made. Interconnected systems render us vulnerable.

56
00:04:02.677 --> 00:04:06.441
Notwithstanding its costs, resilience is essential.

57
00:04:06.741 --> 00:04:14.035
Words like 'wherein' and 'notwithstanding' suit legal and academic writing, but sound stiff in casual speech.

58
00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:18.875
Now read and listen. Living With Self-Made Dangers.

59
00:04:19.175 --> 00:04:24.750
Sociologist Ulrich Beck argued that modernity has created a new kind of society.

60
00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:31.463
In this risk society, the greatest threats are no longer natural but manufactured.

61
00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:38.616
Nuclear accidents, financial crises and climate change are products of human systems.

62
00:04:39.917 --> 00:04:46.374
Globalisation has increased systemic risk, rendering distant economies vulnerable to local failures.

63
00:04:47.683 --> 00:04:54.907
A single disruption may trigger a cascade failure across energy, transport and communication networks.

64
00:04:56.217 --> 00:05:02.732
Risk perception, meanwhile, is shaped as much by media and emotion as by probability.

65
00:05:04.017 --> 00:05:10.080
Precautionary politics and resilience thinking seek to prepare societies for uncertain shocks.

66
00:05:11.383 --> 00:05:17.922
Notwithstanding their costs, such approaches may prove essential to survival in an interconnected age.

67
00:05:19.217 --> 00:05:22.008
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:22.308 --> 00:05:25.132
Danger created by human activity is blank.

69
00:05:25.432 --> 00:05:32.185
A, manufactured risk. B, risk perception. C, resilience thinking.

70
00:05:36.183 --> 00:05:39.588
The answer is A: manufactured risk.

71
00:05:41.083 --> 00:05:43.849
The heavy rain blank the roads impassable.

72
00:05:44.149 --> 00:05:47.338
A, rendered. B, made to. C, gave.

73
00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.593
The answer is A: rendered.

74
00:05:55.083 --> 00:05:57.988
blank the cost, the project went ahead.

75
00:05:58.288 --> 00:06:02.093
A, Notwithstanding. B, Wherein. C, Hence.

76
00:06:06.083 --> 00:06:08.396
The answer is A: Notwithstanding.

77
00:06:09.883 --> 00:06:12.370
A chain reaction of failures is a blank.

78
00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:19.075
A, cascade failure. B, catastrophic risk. C, systemic risk.

79
00:06:23.083 --> 00:06:25.652
The answer is A: cascade failure.

80
00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:30.763
According to the text, what shapes risk perception?

81
00:06:31.063 --> 00:06:37.886
A, only probability. B, media and emotion as well as probability. C, nothing.

82
00:06:41.883 --> 00:06:47.029
The answer is B: media and emotion as well as probability.

83
00:06:48.517 --> 00:06:55.941
Now it's your turn. Part one: Which manufactured risk concerns you most, and how should society respond?

84
00:07:01.241 --> 00:07:05.574
Part two: Use at least two formal expressions in your answer.

85
00:07:11.874 --> 00:07:24.673
Climate change concerns me most, since it renders entire regions vulnerable. Notwithstanding the costs of adaptation, governments should invest in resilience, thereby protecting the most exposed communities.

86
00:07:24.973 --> 00:07:28.157
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:35.819
Great work today! You learned eight new words and formal and literary register (extended).

88
00:07:36.119 --> 00:07:40.258
Today's value is being prepared. Get ready before you go.

89
00:07:40.558 --> 00:07:43.895
See you in Unit twenty-nine. Goodbye!

90
00:07:44.195 --> 00:07:46.708
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

